From fa6b8e763bf848742187d04d0df69ae0f680e9a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 19:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 70/74] vulkan/wsi: Horizon, the layer's size is latched, not
 re-read
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 5 (present modes and scaling)
Why: NWindow::default_* has two authors and one of them is this
 backend. libnx assigns it in _nwindowUpdate, from exactly two callers —
 the bqConnect inside _nwindowConnect, and the bqQueueBuffer inside
 nwindowQueueBuffer — and both carry the BufferQueue's default buffer
 size. Since patch 0054 let an application pick its own extent, what
 this backend puts into the queue is the SWAPCHAIN's size. So
 wsi_horizon_get_extent, which re-read that field on every call, was
 answering vkGetPhysicalDeviceSurfaceCapabilitiesKHR with the size this
 process last registered rather than with the layer's, one connect
 behind.
Evidence: measured on a console 2026-09-07, one process, in the log
 sdmc:/horizon_gpu_tests/vk_wsi.log. vk_wsi/swapchain section G
 presented 5410 frames of a 640x360 swapchain over a 1280x720 layer and
 read currentExtent 1280x720 throughout — nothing had reconnected. The
 next case connected; default_* was refilled with 640x360; and
 vk_wsi/suboptimal, whose capabilities query had said 1280x720, spent
 120 frames with "240 rule disagreements" and then had
 vkCreateSwapchainKHR(1280x720) refused twice with
 VK_ERROR_INITIALIZATION_FAILED — maxImageExtent is the same value, and
 wsi_horizon_surface_create_swapchain checks imageExtent against it.
 170 of 175 checks.
Upstream: no — libnx and NWindow exist nowhere else.

WHAT EACH SIZE IS, because the bug was two of them sharing a field:

  layer      what the compositor scales onto the display. It does not
             move: dock/mode_change watched nwindowGetDimensions,
             NWindow::default_* and appletGetDefaultDisplayResolution
             across four mode changes on 2026-09-07, with a buffer
             queued on every poll so every source was being refreshed,
             and all three said 1280x720 from beginning to end.
  queue      the BufferQueue's default buffer size, which is this
             process's own last registration arriving one connect late.
  buffers    what every registered NvGraphicBuffer is, which is the
             swapchain's extent, because that is what
             nwindowSetDimensions is told at registration.
  swapchain  VkSwapchainCreateInfoKHR::imageExtent, the application's
             choice, bounded above by the layer.

currentExtent and maxImageExtent are the layer's. The latch is taken on
the first query, which is always before this backend has registered
anything — vkGetPhysicalDeviceSurfaceCapabilitiesKHR is how an
application learns what it may create, and create_swapchain asks again
before register_images, which is the only caller of
nwindowSetDimensions.

IT IS ON THE PLATFORM OBJECT, NOT THE SURFACE. vk_wsi destroys a
surface between cases and creates the next over the same NWindow; a
latch per surface would be re-taken after the first swapchain had
already moved the queue. struct wsi_horizon hangs off wsi_device, so
this is state on an explicit context in the same way
wsi_horizon_surface::owner is, and not a file-scope singleton.

THREE THINGS FOLLOW, AND EACH ONE WAS A DEFECT ON ITS OWN:

  - The write into chain->window->default_* on the zero-copy present
    path is gone. It existed to feed the re-read; with the re-read
    gone it feeds nothing, and one unsynchronised write into a libnx
    structure goes with it.
  - wsi_horizon_extent_changed now also ignores the extent this
    surface had registered when the swapchain took the window. That
    is the same one-connect echo seen from the other side: a
    swapchain created at 1280x720 after a 640x360 one is told 640x360
    by every queue until the value catches up, which is neither its
    own extent nor its output_at_create, so every present of a
    correct swapchain came back VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR and an application
    recreating on SUBOPTIMAL would never stop.
  - Releasing the window puts NWindow::width back to the layer's size.
    nwindowSetDimensions at registration set it to the swapchain's,
    libnx keeps it until somebody sets it again, and
    nwindowGetDimensions prefers it over the consumer's value — so a
    shrunk window reached into whatever used the window next,
    including a libnx Framebuffer or a console the application opens
    after Vulkan is done. libnx refuses the call while a slot is
    configured, and the release immediately above is what unconfigures
    them.

What this does NOT do is make a dock visible. Nothing available to this
process reports a stable docked output size; that is measured twice
over in docs/MEASURED-ON-HARDWARE.md and is not what this changes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01YajZtX9CeYzC35x2t1QJJh
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 319 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 252 insertions(+), 67 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index c49b351..e8a3c73 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -220,8 +220,71 @@ static const VkPresentModeKHR wsi_horizon_present_modes[] = {
 /* Surface                                                             */
 /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
 
+/* THE LAYER'S SIZE, OBSERVED ONCE AND THEN KEPT.
+ *
+ * `NWindow::default_*` has two authors, and one of them is this
+ * backend. libnx fills it in `_nwindowUpdate`, from exactly two places:
+ * the `bqConnect` in `_nwindowConnect`, and the `bqQueueBuffer` in
+ * `nwindowQueueBuffer`. Both carry the BufferQueue's default buffer
+ * size — and since patch 0054 let an application choose its own extent,
+ * what this backend registers on the queue is the SWAPCHAIN's size, not
+ * the layer's.
+ *
+ * MEASURED ON A CONSOLE 2026-09-07. `vk_wsi/swapchain` section G
+ * presented 5410 frames of a 640x360 swapchain over a 1280x720 layer;
+ * the surface read 1280x720 throughout, because nothing reconnected.
+ * The next case connected, `default_*` was refilled with 640x360, and
+ * `vk_wsi/suboptimal` — whose capabilities query had said 1280x720 —
+ * spent 120 frames disagreeing with its own surface and then had
+ * `vkCreateSwapchainKHR(1280x720)` refused twice with
+ * `VK_ERROR_INITIALIZATION_FAILED`, because `maxImageExtent` is this
+ * same value. An application that renders at half resolution and later
+ * wants full meets that, not just a test.
+ *
+ * So the query must not re-read the field. It reads it ONCE, before
+ * this backend has registered anything on the window, and keeps the
+ * answer:
+ *
+ *   layer      what the compositor scales to; this, and it does not
+ *              move — `dock/mode_change` watched `nwindowGetDimensions`,
+ *              `NWindow::default_*` and
+ *              `appletGetDefaultDisplayResolution` across four mode
+ *              changes on 2026-09-07 with a buffer queued on every
+ *              poll, and all three said 1280x720 throughout
+ *   queue      the BufferQueue's default buffer size: our own last
+ *              registration, one connect behind. Not the layer
+ *   buffers    what every registered NvGraphicBuffer is: the
+ *              swapchain's extent, because that is what
+ *              nwindowSetDimensions was told at registration
+ *   swapchain  VkSwapchainCreateInfoKHR::imageExtent, the
+ *              application's choice, bounded by the layer
+ *
+ * `currentExtent` and `maxImageExtent` are the layer's, which is what
+ * Vulkan says they are. `minImageExtent` is the floor patch 0054
+ * measured. Nothing published to the application comes from the queue
+ * any more.
+ *
+ * IT IS ON THE PLATFORM OBJECT AND NOT ON THE SURFACE, because the
+ * window outlives any one surface: `vk_wsi` destroys a surface between
+ * cases and creates the next one over the same `NWindow`, and a latch
+ * per surface would be re-taken after the first swapchain had already
+ * moved the queue. This is state on an explicit context — the same
+ * shape as `wsi_horizon_surface::owner` — and not a file-scope
+ * singleton.
+ */
 struct wsi_horizon {
    struct wsi_interface base;
+
+   /* Guards the two fields below: every surface query on this physical
+    * device reads them and the first one writes them. */
+   simple_mtx_t lock;
+
+   /* The window the latch is about. A second window on the same
+    * physical device re-takes it rather than inheriting the first
+    * one's, which is the only thing a one-entry cache has to get
+    * right. */
+   NWindow *layer_window;
+   VkExtent2D layer;
 };
 
 struct wsi_horizon_swapchain;
@@ -245,6 +308,20 @@ struct wsi_horizon_surface {
     */
    simple_mtx_t lock;
    struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *owner;
+
+   /* THE LAST EXTENT REGISTERED ON THE WINDOW THROUGH THIS SURFACE.
+    *
+    * Written under the lock when a swapchain takes the window, read
+    * there too, and handed to the taker as its `registered_before`. It
+    * exists for one reason: the queue's default buffer size answers
+    * with the previous registration for one connect after it, so a
+    * swapchain created at the layer's size after a smaller one is told
+    * by the queue that the output is the smaller size. That is this
+    * process's own voice arriving late, and a resize check that
+    * believed it would report SUBOPTIMAL on a swapchain that is
+    * exactly right. See wsi_horizon_extent_changed.
+    */
+   VkExtent2D last_registered;
 };
 
 static struct wsi_horizon_surface *
@@ -260,6 +337,89 @@ wsi_horizon_window(VkIcdSurfaceBase *base)
    return (NWindow *)wsi_horizon_surface(base)->icd.window;
 }
 
+static struct wsi_horizon *
+wsi_horizon_platform(const struct wsi_device *wsi_device)
+{
+   return (struct wsi_horizon *)wsi_device->wsi[VK_ICD_WSI_PLATFORM_VI];
+}
+
+/* The layer's size: latched on the first ask, returned unchanged
+ * afterwards. See struct wsi_horizon for why it is not re-read.
+ *
+ * The first ask is always before this backend has touched the window.
+ * `vkGetPhysicalDeviceSurfaceCapabilitiesKHR` is how an application
+ * finds out what it may create, and `wsi_horizon_surface_create_
+ * swapchain` asks again before `wsi_horizon_register_images` — which
+ * is the one call that makes `nwindowSetDimensions` and the
+ * registration behind it happen — so the value latched here is the
+ * consumer's own.
+ *
+ * `NWindow::width` is the tell. libnx leaves it zero until somebody
+ * calls `nwindowSetDimensions`, so a non-zero width at latch time means
+ * the window was already configured by something outside this backend,
+ * and `default_*` may be that party's echo rather than the layer's.
+ * That is worth one line in the log and is not worth failing over:
+ * there is no other source. `nwindowGetDimensions` is not one —
+ * it returns `nw->width` when it is set and `nw->default_width`
+ * otherwise, so it is this field with a preference for the producer's
+ * request in front of it.
+ */
+static VkResult
+wsi_horizon_layer_extent(const struct wsi_device *wsi_device,
+                         VkIcdSurfaceBase *base, VkExtent2D *extent)
+{
+   struct wsi_horizon *wsi = wsi_horizon_platform(wsi_device);
+   NWindow *win = wsi_horizon_window(base);
+
+   simple_mtx_lock(&wsi->lock);
+
+   if (wsi->layer_window == win && wsi->layer.width != 0 &&
+       wsi->layer.height != 0) {
+      *extent = wsi->layer;
+      simple_mtx_unlock(&wsi->lock);
+      return VK_SUCCESS;
+   }
+
+   u32 width = win->default_width;
+   u32 height = win->default_height;
+
+   if (width == 0 || height == 0) {
+      /* Nothing has connected yet, so the consumer has said nothing.
+       * libnx's own accessor is the fallback, and on a window this
+       * backend has not touched it reads the same two fields. */
+      Result rc = nwindowGetDimensions(win, &width, &height);
+      if (R_FAILED(rc)) {
+         simple_mtx_unlock(&wsi->lock);
+         mesa_loge("wsi_horizon: nwindowGetDimensions failed: 0x%08x", rc);
+         return VK_ERROR_SURFACE_LOST_KHR;
+      }
+   }
+
+   if (width == 0 || height == 0) {
+      simple_mtx_unlock(&wsi->lock);
+      return VK_ERROR_SURFACE_LOST_KHR;
+   }
+
+   if (win->width != 0 || win->height != 0) {
+      mesa_logw("wsi_horizon: the window already had dimensions %ux%u set on "
+                "it when the layer's size was first asked for; taking %ux%u "
+                "as the layer, which is the consumer's last word and may be "
+                "a previous producer's request",
+                win->width, win->height, width, height);
+   }
+
+   wsi->layer_window = win;
+   wsi->layer = (VkExtent2D) { width, height };
+   *extent = wsi->layer;
+
+   simple_mtx_unlock(&wsi->lock);
+
+   mesa_logi("wsi_horizon: the layer is %ux%u, and that is what this "
+             "surface will report for as long as it exists",
+             extent->width, extent->height);
+   return VK_SUCCESS;
+}
+
 VKAPI_ATTR VkResult VKAPI_CALL
 wsi_CreateViSurfaceNN(VkInstance _instance,
                       const VkViSurfaceCreateInfoNN *pCreateInfo,
@@ -330,43 +490,21 @@ wsi_horizon_surface_get_support(VkIcdSurfaceBase *surface,
    return VK_SUCCESS;
 }
 
-/* The size the COMPOSITOR says this window is, which is also the only
- * size a swapchain over it may have: nwindowSetDimensions refuses to
- * change the window while buffers are registered, and every registered
- * buffer must match.
+/* The size of the OUTPUT every surface query answers with, which is
+ * also the largest a swapchain over this window may be.
  *
- * NOT nwindowGetDimensions, and the difference matters. That returns
- * NWindow::width when it is set, which is whatever the last
- * nwindowSetDimensions asked for — and this backend is what calls it,
- * so asking would be reading back our own request. NWindow::default_*
- * is the consumer's answer: libnx fills it from the BufferQueue's
- * output at connect and at every queue. It is what changes when the
- * console is docked and the display becomes 1920x1080.
+ * One line, because the question is settled in one place now:
+ * wsi_horizon_layer_extent latches the layer before this backend has
+ * moved anything, and every query reads the latch. The version this
+ * replaced re-read NWindow::default_* on every call, which is the
+ * field this backend's own registrations end up in — see the comment
+ * on struct wsi_horizon for the measurement that ended that.
  */
 static VkResult
-wsi_horizon_get_extent(VkIcdSurfaceBase *surface, VkExtent2D *extent)
+wsi_horizon_get_extent(const struct wsi_device *wsi_device,
+                       VkIcdSurfaceBase *surface, VkExtent2D *extent)
 {
-   NWindow *win = wsi_horizon_window(surface);
-   u32 width = win->default_width;
-   u32 height = win->default_height;
-
-   if (width == 0 || height == 0) {
-      /* Nothing has been queued yet and the connect told us nothing.
-       * Fall back on the window's own answer, which is what t_display
-       * measured as 1280x720 on this hardware. */
-      Result rc = nwindowGetDimensions(win, &width, &height);
-      if (R_FAILED(rc)) {
-         mesa_loge("wsi_horizon: nwindowGetDimensions failed: 0x%08x", rc);
-         return VK_ERROR_SURFACE_LOST_KHR;
-      }
-   }
-
-   if (width == 0 || height == 0)
-      return VK_ERROR_SURFACE_LOST_KHR;
-
-   extent->width = width;
-   extent->height = height;
-   return VK_SUCCESS;
+   return wsi_horizon_layer_extent(wsi_device, surface, extent);
 }
 
 /* How many images the application must leave the queue, plus one.
@@ -412,7 +550,7 @@ wsi_horizon_surface_get_capabilities(VkIcdSurfaceBase *surface,
                                      VkSurfaceCapabilitiesKHR *caps)
 {
    VkExtent2D extent;
-   VkResult result = wsi_horizon_get_extent(surface, &extent);
+   VkResult result = wsi_horizon_get_extent(wsi_device, surface, &extent);
    if (result != VK_SUCCESS)
       return result;
 
@@ -648,7 +786,7 @@ wsi_horizon_surface_get_present_rectangles(VkIcdSurfaceBase *surface,
    VK_OUTARRAY_MAKE_TYPED(VkRect2D, out, pRects, pRectCount);
 
    VkExtent2D extent;
-   VkResult result = wsi_horizon_get_extent(surface, &extent);
+   VkResult result = wsi_horizon_get_extent(wsi_device, surface, &extent);
    if (result != VK_SUCCESS)
       return result;
 
@@ -802,6 +940,11 @@ struct wsi_horizon_swapchain {
     * `extent`, which is the application's own choice and is allowed to
     * differ from the output for as long as the application likes. */
    VkExtent2D output_at_create;
+   /* What this surface had registered on the window when this swapchain
+    * took it, so the resize check can tell the queue's one-connect lag
+    * from the consumer speaking. Zero for the first swapchain over a
+    * window, which matches nothing. */
+   VkExtent2D registered_before;
    const struct wsi_horizon_format *format;
    uint32_t swap_interval;
    bool said_short_copy;      /* the fallback's narrowed-row warning, once */
@@ -1181,6 +1324,11 @@ wsi_horizon_claim_window(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
    surface->owner = chain;
    chain->presentable = true;
 
+   /* Handed over under the same lock that publishes the ownership, so
+    * the pair cannot be read half-updated by the next claim. */
+   chain->registered_before = surface->last_registered;
+   surface->last_registered = chain->extent;
+
    simple_mtx_unlock(&surface->lock);
    return VK_SUCCESS;
 }
@@ -1228,6 +1376,33 @@ wsi_horizon_release_window(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain)
             mesa_logw("wsi_horizon: nwindowReleaseBuffers failed: 0x%08x", rc);
          }
       }
+
+      /* AND THE WINDOW IS LEFT THE SIZE THE LAYER IS.
+       *
+       * nwindowSetDimensions at registration set NWindow::width to this
+       * swapchain's extent, and libnx keeps that until somebody sets it
+       * again: nwindowGetDimensions prefers it over the consumer's
+       * value, and nwindowDequeueBuffer asks the queue for buffers of
+       * that size. Leaving a shrunk window behind is how one swapchain
+       * reached into the next thing to use the window — including a
+       * libnx Framebuffer or a console the application opens after
+       * Vulkan is done with it.
+       *
+       * It is only allowed now: libnx refuses this call while any slot
+       * is configured, and the release above is what unconfigures them.
+       * A failure is logged and not propagated — the swapchain is gone
+       * either way, and there is no caller left to tell. */
+      VkExtent2D layer;
+      if (wsi_horizon_layer_extent(chain->base.wsi, &surface->icd.base,
+                                   &layer) == VK_SUCCESS) {
+         Result rc = nwindowSetDimensions(chain->window, layer.width,
+                                          layer.height);
+         if (R_FAILED(rc)) {
+            mesa_logw("wsi_horizon: the window would not go back to the "
+                      "layer's %ux%u: 0x%08x", layer.width, layer.height, rc);
+         }
+      }
+
       surface->owner = NULL;
    }
    chain->presentable = false;
@@ -1495,6 +1670,29 @@ wsi_horizon_extent_changed(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain)
    if (width == chain->extent.width && height == chain->extent.height)
       return false;   /* our own request, echoed back */
 
+   /* AND NOT THE ONE BEFORE IT EITHER, which is the same voice arriving
+    * one connect late.
+    *
+    * MEASURED 2026-09-07: a 640x360 swapchain leaves the queue's
+    * default buffer size at 640x360, and the next swapchain — created
+    * at the layer's 1280x720, registered at 1280x720, presenting
+    * 1280x720 — is told 640x360 by every queue until the value catches
+    * up. Neither `extent` nor `output_at_create`, so the test above
+    * passes it through and every present of a correct swapchain comes
+    * back VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR. An application that recreates on
+    * SUBOPTIMAL would do it forever.
+    *
+    * `registered_before` is what the surface had registered when this
+    * swapchain took the window, so this excludes exactly the lag and
+    * nothing else. A consumer that really did change the output to the
+    * previous swapchain's size in the meantime would be missed, which
+    * needs the display to have been resized to a value this process
+    * chose earlier, on a platform where four measured mode changes
+    * resized it by nothing at all. */
+   if (width == chain->registered_before.width &&
+       height == chain->registered_before.height)
+      return false;
+
    return width != chain->output_at_create.width ||
           height != chain->output_at_create.height;
 }
@@ -2234,39 +2432,21 @@ wsi_horizon_present_zero_copy(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
        * a concurrent present on the fallback path,
        * so this adds no hazard that was not already there and is not
        * this backend's to fix: the structure belongs to libnx. */
-      /* BUT NOT WHEN THAT IS OUR OWN VOICE COMING BACK.
+      /* AND NOTHING IS WRITTEN BACK INTO THE WINDOW, which is the
+       * whole of what this used to do.
        *
-       * The queue's default buffer size has two authors: the consumer,
-       * on a display mode change, and this backend, through
-       * nwindowSetDimensions at registration — which sets it to the
-       * SWAPCHAIN's extent. While every swapchain matched the layer
-       * those were the same number and writing it back was harmless.
+       * It wrote the queue's answer into NWindow::default_* whenever it
+       * differed from this swapchain's extent, so that
+       * wsi_horizon_get_extent — which read that field on every call —
+       * would see a display mode change. The field was the wrong place
+       * to keep the answer and re-reading it was the wrong way to ask:
+       * see struct wsi_horizon. The surface queries now read a latch
+       * taken before this backend touched anything, so there is nothing
+       * for this to feed and one unsynchronised write into a libnx
+       * structure goes away with it.
        *
-       * Since patch 0054 they need not be. MEASURED ON A CONSOLE
-       * 2026-08-24, with a 640x360 swapchain on a 1280x720 layer:
-       *
-       *   note G: ... the surface now reports 640x360;
-       *        appletGetOperationMode() went 0 -> 0
-       *
-       * Nothing had docked. The swapchain's own extent had been written
-       * into NWindow::default_*, which wsi_horizon_get_extent reads, so
-       * VkSurfaceCapabilitiesKHR::currentExtent — and with it
-       * maxImageExtent — shrank to the size of the last swapchain and
-       * stayed there. An application that rendered small once could
-       * never ask for native again, and one that queried the surface
-       * after being told it was suboptimal would be handed the wrong
-       * answer twice over.
-       *
-       * A value equal to what this backend asked for teaches nothing:
-       * either nothing has changed, or the consumer happens to agree.
-       * A value that DIFFERS is the consumer's, which is the only case
-       * patch 0040 added this write for — a mode change is exactly when
-       * the two stop matching. */
-      if (out.width != chain->extent.width ||
-          out.height != chain->extent.height) {
-         chain->window->default_width = out.width;
-         chain->window->default_height = out.height;
-      }
+       * `chain->reported` above is where the queue's answer lives now,
+       * and wsi_horizon_extent_changed is its only reader. */
    }
 
    return VK_SUCCESS;
@@ -2633,7 +2813,7 @@ wsi_horizon_surface_create_swapchain(VkIcdSurfaceBase *icd_surface,
       return VK_ERROR_INITIALIZATION_FAILED;
 
    VkExtent2D extent;
-   result = wsi_horizon_get_extent(icd_surface, &extent);
+   result = wsi_horizon_get_extent(wsi_device, icd_surface, &extent);
    if (result != VK_SUCCESS)
       return result;
 
@@ -3060,6 +3240,10 @@ wsi_horizon_init_wsi(struct wsi_device *wsi_device,
       return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY;
    }
 
+   simple_mtx_init(&wsi->lock, mtx_plain);
+   wsi->layer_window = NULL;
+   wsi->layer = (VkExtent2D) { 0, 0 };
+
    wsi->base.get_support = wsi_horizon_surface_get_support;
    wsi->base.get_capabilities2 = wsi_horizon_surface_get_capabilities2;
    wsi->base.get_formats = wsi_horizon_surface_get_formats;
@@ -3084,5 +3268,6 @@ wsi_horizon_finish_wsi(struct wsi_device *wsi_device,
       return;
 
    wsi_device->wsi[VK_ICD_WSI_PLATFORM_VI] = NULL;
+   simple_mtx_destroy(&wsi->lock);
    vk_free(alloc, wsi);
 }
